  ],
  "text" : "In Python, think of a process as a kitchen.\nHere, threads are cooks,\nand they all share the kitchen's memory.\nThe knife is the gill,\nthe global interpreter lock.\nWhoever holds the knife is what\nruns the Python code while everyone else waits.\nThis is why threading a CPU loop makes it no faster.\nFour threads with one knife,\nyou have one course worth of work still.\nBut a cook puts the knife down to wait on the oven.\nThat could be an I/O, a request,\na file read, a sleep.\nThe gill is released while the thread waits,\nso another thread can run.\nSame for an appliance.\nThink of NumPy, image decoding, a tokenizer.\nBut when threads don't help, ask one thing.\nIs this thread waiting, or is it running Python?\nIf it's waiting, then threads work.\nBut if it's running Python, you need a second kitchen\nor another process."
}
